title The boundary written from both sides
prompt The baton, and the card's own named next door -- the boundary sentence at both front doors.
rota lap 4278, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes. The fixed seat gave the lap its shape: a boundary between two pieces carries a contract, and the contract is written from BOTH sides, the two families mirroring each other line for line. Two prose rooms are two pieces, and one sentence at each front door is that mirror in prose rather than in tests. The cardinal seat's question -- would running this again change anything more? -- reads clean here: both edits are one paragraph inserted at a named anchor, and a second run finds the anchor already replaced.
think Opened the cold roster before touching anything, and it answered 188 guards, 1,854s, three red. Reds first, so the constructive door waited.
think dayshelf_merge red at 0s with its own repair printed on its face -- this clone had never armed the driver. The card already named it: seven peer ships each want one install_hooks run. That is an environment precondition rather than a tree red, the same class as an uninitialised vendor/, so it earned no ledger row.
think standing_equipment red only because it reports the other two. One reporter, two subjects.
think reds_citation was the real one, and it printed the whole finding on one line: disagree fold construction/archive/REDS-fold-recital.md:520 claim=%614 path=rows-613.
think Read the linked shelf's own headline before believing either side. REDS %613 is the plant row; the recital line claimed %614. So the number was wrong and the path was right.
think git log -S on the exact string named the commit that wrote it, and the diff is the whole lesson: the line already read %613 and was rewritten to %614 in the same diff that added its own %614/%615 line. A lap whose unshared rows renumbered on its rebase swept the new number through the file and caught a peer's already-correct citation, because the peer's line spelled the same digits.
think derived-spine already records one blind sed that rewrote a peer's row and two upstream passages. This is the second firing of that shape, and it lands on the recital -- the one page where every ship's folds sit side by side, so a bare number is ambiguous by construction.
think Checked whether --scoped could have hidden it. reds_citation is tier lap and absent from tools/fixtures/s/standing_equipment_scope_map.sh, and the runner's law is that an unmapped guard always runs, so a scoped pass would have run it too. What a guard cannot do is run before a commit, which is where a sweep is still one line.
think Fetched before booking, read 619 from the anointed spine, then found the local spine reds not_monotone -- my tree was behind and %618 was upstream. Pulled with autostash, re-read the spine with my own row stashed out to prove 619 was genuinely free, and it was.
think The constructive half is the brief's proposal 2, the only one of its three that is agent-doable. Items 1 and 3 seat a boundary and molt three pairs, which are Keaton's word.
think Wrote each door's sentence to lead with its OWN reader and then name the other two rooms, so the two sides mirror rather than cross-reference. Measured both before and after rather than trusting the shape.
obs The lap's two halves turned out to be one subject seen twice. The red is a sweep that could not tell its own citation from a peer's; the door work is a boundary made legible from both sides. Both are the same failure of a shared surface where nobody wrote down which side owns what.
obs The rule's answer to the red was already written and simply not reached for: the stamp is the key. A renumber sweep matching 20260908.053644 rather than %613 finds exactly one line and leaves the peer's alone. I booked the instance and named the habit change as Keaton's, because how every ship renumbers is not a docs lane's call.
obs The card had 53 bytes of headroom, so the elder PETRICHOR account shelved whole and the new block was trimmed six times to land at 40,958 under 40,960. Writing to a bound is its own small discipline.
think The send's rebase moved twice under me and both moves were peers being right. A peer published %619 and %620 while I measured, so my unshared row derived above them to %621; and the same one-character recital repair had already landed upstream in 34dce2e39, so my edit withdrew whole and only the row remains mine. That is upstream %619's own lesson -- two ships repairing one visible red inside an hour -- arriving from the other side on the very lap I booked it.
think The rebase also carried my pin copy back over a peer's fold of %617, which would have un-folded their work. Dropped it by name after proving it stands on their shelf REDS-a-safe-tool-that-sent-by-default-rows-617.md.
think With the pin at its bound and every remaining row OPEN, the only foldable rows were the peer's freshly published %619 and my own %621. Folded mine: the only bytes a lap may move without asking are its own.
obs Water's cardinal question answered honestly: nothing this lap wrote is a transform that could run twice. Both door edits replace a named anchor, the ledger row is an insert above a named row, and a second run of either would refuse to find its anchor rather than duplicate.
think The hot pass found a third thing, and it was mine: shelving the elder PETRICHOR account whole carried its link `archive/20260908-051419_...` down into `construction/archive/`, where the `archive/` segment is one level too deep. fold_shelf_link named it with the single correction on its face and fold_shelf_link_repoint applied exactly that -- accrete keeps the words, and a link's depth is read from where the shelf now stands rather than from where its text came.
obs Shelving an account whole is the one move that reliably plants this fault, because the elder text was written at the card's depth and the shelf sits one directory down. The guard exists for that shape and caught it before the commit, which is where the repair is one line.
